# Strategy Pattern

## Classification

```text
Gang of Four (GoF) Design Pattern
Category: Behavioral Pattern
```

Behavioral patterns focus on:

> How objects collaborate and execute behavior.

Strategy specifically addresses:

> "How can we switch between different behaviors/algorithms without modifying existing code?"

---

# Why Strategy Pattern Was Introduced?

Consider a payment service:

```java
if(type.equals("UPI")) {
   ...
}
else if(type.equals("CARD")) {
   ...
}
else if(type.equals("APPLE_PAY")) {
   ...
}
```

Initially fine.

As business grows:

```java
if(...)
else if(...)
else if(...)
else if(...)
```

## Typical Symptoms (Most Important First)

### 1. Multiple Responsibilities In One Class ⭐

```java
PaymentService
```

owns:

- UPI payment logic
- Card payment logic
- Wallet payment logic
- Apple Pay logic

Each payment type changes for different reasons.

This is usually the biggest design smell and often the primary reason Strategy emerges.

---

### 2. Tight Coupling

```java
PaymentService
```

must know every payment implementation.

Whenever a new payment method is introduced, the service becomes more aware of internal details.

---

### 3. Large Conditional Logic

```java
if(...)
else if(...)
else if(...)
else if(...)
```

Decision logic keeps growing as new behaviors are added.

This is usually the most visible symptom.

---

### 4. Difficult Testing

Testing one behavior often requires navigating through unrelated behaviors living in the same class.

Each payment implementation cannot be tested independently.

---

## Natural Consequence

Because of the above problems:

- Existing code gets modified frequently
- Regression risk increases
- OCP gets violated

Strategy Pattern addresses these issues by moving each behavior into its own class and using polymorphism for execution.
```

# Core Idea

Encapsulate each behavior into its own class and expose a common contract.

Instead of:

```java
if(paymentType == UPI)
```

Use:

```java
PaymentStrategy strategy
```

and let polymorphism decide the implementation.

---

# Structure

```java
interface PaymentStrategy {
    void pay();
}
```

Implementations:

```java
UpiStrategy
CardStrategy
ApplePayStrategy
GooglePayStrategy
```

Usage:

```java
PaymentStrategy strategy =
       strategyMap.get(type);

strategy.pay();
```

---

# Runtime Behavior Selection

A common misconception:

> Strategy enables runtime behavior selection.

Many developers think:

```text
If-Else does not support runtime selection.
```

Wrong.

This also happens at runtime:

```java
if(type.equals("UPI"))
```

The real benefit is not runtime selection.

The real benefit is:

```text
Behavior selection through polymorphism
instead of large conditional logic.
```

---

# How Runtime Decision Happens?

User selects:

```text
APPLE_PAY
```

Request:

```json
{
  "paymentMethod": "APPLE_PAY"
}
```

Lookup:

```java
PaymentStrategy strategy =
      strategyMap.get("APPLE_PAY");
```

Result:

```java
ApplePayStrategy
```

Execution:

```java
strategy.pay();
```

JVM performs dynamic dispatch and invokes the correct implementation.

---

# Main Problem It Solves

Before:

```java
Decision Logic
+
Business Logic
```

mixed together.

Example:

```java
if(type.equals("UPI")) {
    // UPI implementation
}

if(type.equals("CARD")) {
    // CARD implementation
}
```

After:

```java
strategy.pay();
```

Decision and implementation become separate concerns.

---

# SOLID Principles Involved

## SRP (Single Responsibility Principle)

Before:

```java
PaymentService
```

owns:

- UPI payment
- Card payment
- Wallet payment
- Apple Pay payment

Multiple responsibilities.

After:

```java
UpiStrategy
```

owns only UPI logic.

```java
CardStrategy
```

owns only Card logic.

Each class has one reason to change.

✅ Better SRP

---

## OCP (Open Closed Principle)

Open for extension.

Closed for modification.

Before:

```java
if(...)
else if(...)
else if(...)
```

New payment?

Modify existing code.

After:

```java
class GooglePayStrategy
     implements PaymentStrategy
```

Add new behavior.

Existing tested implementations remain untouched.

✅ Better OCP

---

# A Very Important Realization

OCP does NOT mean:

```text
No code changes anywhere.
```

Some registration/configuration change is expected.

Example:

```java
strategies.put(
  "GOOGLE_PAY",
  new GooglePayStrategy()
);
```

or

```java
@Component
class GooglePayStrategy
```

This is acceptable.

Why?

Because business logic remains unchanged.

---

# Spring Configuration Analogy

Suppose database URL changes.

```properties
spring.datasource.url=db-server-1
```

becomes

```properties
spring.datasource.url=db-server-2
```

Did application logic change?

❌ No

Did Repository code change?

❌ No

Did Service code change?

❌ No

Only configuration changed.

Similarly:

```java
GooglePayStrategy
```

registration is considered an extension/configuration change.

Not a business logic modification.

---

# Extension vs Modification

This is the heart of OCP.

Bad:

```java
Modify tested code repeatedly.
```

Example:

```java
if(type.equals("GOOGLE_PAY"))
```

added inside existing service.

Good:

```java
Add new strategy class.
```

Example:

```java
GooglePayStrategy
```

Existing tested behavior remains untouched.

---

# Loose Coupling

Before:

```java
PaymentService
```

knows every payment implementation.

After:

```java
PaymentService
```

knows only:

```java
PaymentStrategy
```

It depends on an abstraction.

Not a concrete implementation.

✅ Loose coupling

✅ Easier testing

✅ Easier extension

---

# Polymorphism Is The Foundation

Strategy Pattern heavily relies on:

```text
Polymorphism
```

Example:

```java
PaymentStrategy strategy
```

can point to:

```java
new UpiStrategy()
```

or

```java
new CardStrategy()
```

or

```java
new ApplePayStrategy()
```

At runtime JVM calls the appropriate implementation.

This is dynamic dispatch.

