# Common Misconceptions

## Myth 1

```text
Strategy exists to remove all if-else statements.
```

False.

A small if condition is perfectly fine.

Purpose is:

```text
Encapsulate changing behavior.
```

---

## Myth 2

```text
Strategy is mainly for performance.
```

False.

Purpose is maintainability and extensibility.

---

## Myth 3

```text
Strategy completely eliminates configuration changes.
```

False.

Extension/configuration changes are acceptable.

Business logic modification is what we try to avoid.

---

# When To Use Strategy?

Use when:

✅ Multiple implementations exist

✅ Behavior varies

✅ New behaviors will be added frequently

✅ Large if-else blocks are growing

✅ Runtime behavior selection is required

✅ OCP and SRP improvements are desired

---

# When Not To Use Strategy?

Do not introduce Strategy for:

```java
if(user == null)
```

or

```java
if(amount <= 0)
```

Small conditional logic is perfectly acceptable.

Use Strategy when behavior variation is expected to grow.

---

# Interview Definition

> Strategy Pattern is a GoF Behavioral Design Pattern that encapsulates a family of interchangeable algorithms or behaviors behind a common interface. It relies on polymorphism to select the appropriate behavior at runtime, improves SRP by separating responsibilities, supports OCP through extension rather than modification, reduces large conditional logic, and keeps business logic loosely coupled from concrete implementations.

